WEBVTT

1
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:21.894
Sorry if I scared you, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:22.695 --> 00:00:23.936
It's only me, Melissa.

3
00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:28.879
My aunt just got back from a trip to Italy, and she brought me this feather mask from

4
00:00:28.959 --> 00:00:31.140
Venice. It's pretty cool, huh?

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.543
Did you know that the city of Venice is famous for its fancy masks?

6
00:00:36.523 --> 00:00:38.205
But that's not all it's famous for.

7
00:00:39.185 --> 00:00:45.510
Venice is often called the "Floating City" because it was built on a group of 118 small islands.

8
00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:50.013
It looks like the city floats magically on top of the water!

9
00:00:50.954 --> 00:00:53.916
We learned all about Venice this year during one of Mr.

10
00:00:53.976 --> 00:00:55.277
Fay's landmarks lessons.

11
00:00:56.338 --> 00:01:01.887
Located in a lagoon in the Adriatic Sea, Venice used to be a powerful maritime republic.

12
00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:07.712
Many people think it's still the most beautiful—and amazing—city in the world.

13
00:01:09.373 --> 00:01:13.617
Historians believe that Venice's earliest residents were fishermen and salt workers.

14
00:01:14.558 --> 00:01:20.883
Then, during the fifth century, others began fleeing to the islands to escape raids by barbarians.

15
00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:29.037
Over time the islands in the lagoon united to form a republic, and by 727, the first doge,

16
00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:31.578
or duke, had been selected to govern it.

17
00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:38.740
From the ninth through the fifteenth centuries, the Republic of Venice flourished, serving as Europe's main center of

18
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:44.182
trade—the place where silk, spices, and other valuable goods arrived from Asia.

19
00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:47.803
Many wealthy Venetians were patrons of the arts.

20
00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:57.039
That encouraged glassmakers, painters, musicians, and of course, mask-makers to create many beautiful works during this time.

21
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.062
By the end of the fifteenth century, however, the Republic of Venice began to decline.

22
00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.764
Ottoman Turks had been seizing lots of nearby land.

23
00:02:07.685 --> 00:02:14.349
Plus, other European countries started to discover new trade routes, breaking up Venice's empire along the coast.

24
00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:20.292
The Republic finally collapsed in 1797 when Napoleon's army conquered it.

25
00:02:21.148 --> 00:02:25.953
Then, in 1886, Venice became part of the Kingdom of Italy.

26
00:02:26.874 --> 00:02:34.140
If you ask me, the most interesting thing about Venice—besides its fancy masks—is the city's unusual geography.

27
00:02:35.001 --> 00:02:40.946
The archipelago sits in a shallow lagoon with more than two hundred canals linking together to form a

28
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:42.728
huge waterway system.

29
00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:46.632
A row of sandbanks shelters the lagoon from the open sea.

30
00:02:47.492 --> 00:02:51.994
Openings in these sandbanks allow traffic (and tides) to flow in and out of the city.

31
00:02:53.615 --> 00:02:58.436
If you've ever been to Venice, you probably know that there are only two ways to get around—by

32
00:02:58.517 --> 00:02:59.617
boat or by foot.

33
00:03:00.497 --> 00:03:04.859
Because of its narrow streets and canals, no cars are permitted in the city.

34
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:08.861
Mr. Fay told us that traveling by boat is lots of fun.

35
00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:14.703
The Grand Canal, which is Venice's longest waterway, cuts through the heart of the city.

36
00:03:15.683 --> 00:03:24.329
It's constantly busy, filled with all kinds of noisy traffic—motor-powered water buses and taxis, mail boats, fruit barges,

37
00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.412
even fire, police, ambulance, and garbage boats!

38
00:03:29.453 --> 00:03:35.697
The most famous type of boat in Venice is definitely the gondola, the black, flat-bottomed boat that's considered

39
00:03:35.718 --> 00:03:36.818
a symbol of the city.

40
00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:44.624
Propelled by gondoliers, or drivers who use long poles to steer, gondolas are really popular with tourists.

41
00:03:46.232 --> 00:03:50.975
But we learned something sad about these ancient boats—there are only a few hundred left.

42
00:03:51.936 --> 00:03:57.479
Gondolas are very expensive to build, and today very few people know how to carve them.

43
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:05.044
For pedestrians, Venice has nearly four hundred canal bridges, which means you can get nearly anywhere in Venice

44
00:04:05.084 --> 00:04:05.745
by walking!

45
00:04:06.725 --> 00:04:10.368
Many of the bridges are marble, sculpted hundreds of years ago.

46
00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.223
Lots of people enjoy sightseeing from the Rialto Bridge with its great view of the traffic in the

47
00:04:16.263 --> 00:04:16.943
Grand Canal.

48
00:04:17.804 --> 00:04:20.866
Another famous bridge is called the Bridge of Sighs.

49
00:04:21.806 --> 00:04:27.429
The poet, Lord Byron, named it that because convicts used to cross it on their way to imprisonment.

50
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:34.574
As Bryon imagined it, the convicts would sigh with sadness as they took one last look at lovely

51
00:04:34.654 --> 00:04:36.615
Venice—and their freedom!

52
00:04:40.264 --> 00:04:45.968
Other popular tourist sites include the Doge's Palace, which was the doges' home and the political center of

53
00:04:46.028 --> 00:04:47.909
life in Venice, and St.

54
00:04:47.969 --> 00:04:49.089
Mark's Basilica.

55
00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.952
St. Mark's is an old church that once served as the doges' private chapel.

56
00:04:54.853 --> 00:04:56.654
In front of the basilica is St.

57
00:04:56.694 --> 00:05:01.017
Mark's Plaza, a square lined with historic buildings, cafes, and shops.

58
00:05:02.057 --> 00:05:06.400
The square is nearly always packed with tourists, as well as hungry pigeons!

59
00:05:07.392 --> 00:05:11.274
I asked my aunt why masks are such a popular souvenir from Venice.

60
00:05:12.355 --> 00:05:17.478
She explained that masks have probably been part of Venice's history since the thirteenth century.

61
00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:23.262
Historians believe Venetians sometimes wore them to disguise their social class and gender.

62
00:05:24.262 --> 00:05:30.726
Masks were also worn during Carnival, the Christian festival that takes place before the holy season of Lent.

63
00:05:32.387 --> 00:05:37.829
In fact, people today still celebrate Carnival in Venice by wearing elaborate masks.

64
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:44.351
Mr. Fay said you can see some really um unusual ones there!

65
00:05:45.431 --> 00:05:48.092
Today tourism is Venice's main industry.

66
00:05:48.872 --> 00:05:54.594
All those tourists help to keep Venice's economy healthy, but they've also brought problems to the city, like

67
00:05:54.674 --> 00:05:57.355
huge crowds and very high prices.

68
00:05:58.475 --> 00:06:02.296
Another serious problem for the "Floating City" is that it's sinking!

69
00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:09.392
The city has had several devastating floods, and with the sea level rising in the Adriatic Sea, officials

70
00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:11.353
worry that flooding will grow even worse.

71
00:06:12.414 --> 00:06:17.678
So city planners have halted development and relocated historic buildings to higher ground.

72
00:06:18.598 --> 00:06:25.263
And engineers have been working for decades to construct an elaborate barrier system with huge metal gates to

73
00:06:25.323 --> 00:06:26.363
hold back the tides.

74
00:06:28.165 --> 00:06:34.441
I think lots of people want to make sure that Venice—with all its unique beauty—is preserved for future

75
00:06:34.481 --> 00:06:37.002
generations. According to Mr.

76
00:06:37.042 --> 00:06:42.005
Fay, it's not only one of the oldest tourist sites in the world, but it has also captured

77
00:06:42.025 --> 00:06:46.467
the imagination of poets, painters, and filmmakers for centuries.

78
00:06:47.448 --> 00:06:55.772
The poet Henry Wadsworth Longfellow once called it a "white phantom city, whose untrodden streets are rivers and

79
00:06:55.812 --> 00:06:59.374
whose pavements are the shifting shadows of palaces."

80
00:07:00.515 --> 00:07:02.876
That's a pretty awesome description, don't you think?

81
00:07:03.916 --> 00:07:08.522
Ooh! I hear my brother coming upstairs, and I want to scare him with my feather mask!

82
00:07:09.383 --> 00:07:10.925
See you later, Little Fox readers!
